  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBroadly, forecast expectations have not changed in any significant \nway. The most plausible outcome in the short term is for another \nround of showers and non-severe thunderstorms to move into the area\nlate afternoon/early tonight and exit early Thursday morning. \nThis is far from a certain forecast, however.\n\nThe challenge continues to be timing rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms across the area, mostly south of a diffuse quasi- \nstationary front that extended northeast to southwest through the\nnorthwest forecast area this afternoon. Scattered rain showers \nare already ongoing across the forecast area. More widespread \nongoing convection is noted across IA/MO/IL ahead of upper PV \nforcing depicted by both satellite water vapor imagery and short \nrange model guidance.\n\nConvection allowing guidance has shown significant run to run \nvariability, which suggests large forecast sensitivity to antecedent \nconvective trends. It is interesting to note that even the ECMWF \ndeterministic model hints at this; it erodes the PV max (and \ntherefore organized upper forcing) as it moves east towards Lower \nMI. This is likely due to modeled deep moist convection and \nattendant mid-level latent heat release that has the effect of \ndecreasing both static stability and cyclonic vorticity (the \ningredients of PV) at the upper levels. The substantial influence of \ndiabatic forcing we see here amidst weak synoptic forcing is what \nreally hurts the predictability of this forecast.\n\nAs noted previously, conditional instability is not favorable for \nstrong storms and will be further limited by ongoing precipitation. \n12Z HREF guidance does indicate a continued chance of thunderstorms \novernight trailing off closer to sunrise. Another item of concern is \nlocally heavy precipitation towards I-94. This is advertised by HREF \nLPMM. Having said that, the 12Z REFS is completely different, \nshowing greatest (but far less impressive) precipitation amounts \ntowards Clare County. This all speaks to the low forecast certainty \nwe can expect even in the first 24 hours, but there is the possibility\nof localized significant rain, leading to at least some nuisance \nflooding overnight.\n\nThursday afternoon now looks a little drier than before with the \nfocus for most precipitation, including thunderstorms, shifted east \ntowards southeast Lower Michigan. But again, this will depend a lot \non convective evolution beforehand. \n\nAfter Thursday, broad upper troughing will slowly migrate from the \nwestern Great Lakes to the central Great Lakes with perhaps some \nflattening by the beginning of next week. As noted before, the \ntiming of convection will remain uncertain, yielding a now familiar \nrepeating theme of 20-40 PoPs in the extended forecast. It appears \nunlikely that we will be seeing much in the way of strong/severe \nconvection or significant areal flooding.",
